Build the tool from generated Go source with buildGoModule and a vendorHash of
null, the same shape the http example uses. Name that artifact boardcore.

The board file and the column names are configuration, not source. The
compiled program reads them from the environment variables BOARD_FILE and
BOARD_COLUMNS, so editing either value in the program flows through compile
without regenerating the source. BOARD_COLUMNS is one string holding the
column names as the program wrote them.

Put the tool on PATH through a second artifact named board, built with
writeShellApplication: its text exports those two variables and then execs
${artifact.boardcore}/bin/boardcore with the caller's arguments, and
home.packages holds that wrapper. Do not use a systemd service and do not set
a session variable.

Render the board with plain ANSI escape codes. No curses library and no
external TUI library.
